# Register Modeling & Logging (shared regmodel libraries)

Hand-written SystemC/TLM register models anywhere in this repository -- regardless
of subsystem (SMC, SEP, VP, or any other system being modelled) -- are not code-generated. To avoid
re-deriving the same RO/WO/RW1C/woset/lock-gated masking logic independently in
every file (and the policy drift that causes), new or edited register access
code **must** use the shared, generic, dependency-free library
`common/include/reg_access.h` (namespace `regmodel`) instead of hand-rolling the
mask arithmetic inline.

The library is width-generic (`Register8/16/32/64`, or `Register<Word>`) and
framework-free (depends only on `<cstdint>`/`<functional>`), so this rule
applies to any hand-written model regardless of subsystem or register width.

## Scope: which models this covers

- **Any hand-written register model** in this repository -- SMC peripherals
  (`i3c_controller`, `reset_unit`, `uart`, ...), `smc_fabric`, SEP models,
  VP platforms, and any future subsystem -- must use `regmodel` for masking logic.
- **Models built on an external framework** (e.g. SEP peripherals that use the
  CSML library, `csml_reg`/`csml_bitfield`/`csml_memory`) keep using that
  framework's mechanisms; do not migrate them onto `regmodel` wholesale.
  However, when such a model drops to raw C++ for a semantic that the framework
  does not cover (a W1C/woset/lock-gated callback body, for example), use the
  `regmodel` free functions for the masking arithmetic rather than re-deriving
  it inline.
- **New models with no framework** should default to `regmodel`.

Add `common/include` to the target's `target_include_directories` and
`#include "reg_access.h"`.

## Semantic write patterns: use the free functions

```cpp
#include "reg_access.h"
using namespace regmodel;

// W1C
case FOO_INTR_STATUS:
    s.intr_status = apply_w1c(s.intr_status, data, W1C_MASK); break;
// woset (sticky lock)
case FOO_LOCK:
    s.lock = apply_woset(s.lock, data); break;
// lock-gated read-modify-write
case FOO_CONFIG:
    s.config = apply_lock_gated(s.config, data, s.lock); break;
```

Do NOT re-derive these inline (`s.x &= ~(data & MASK)`, `(data & ~lock) | ...`).

## Plain RO/WO/RW registers: prefer `regmodel::Register<Word>`

For a register whose behavior is just mask-on-read / mask-merge-on-write,
declare it as a `Register32` (or the width-appropriate alias) rather than a
bare integer decoded by hand -- the mask contract becomes part of the object:

```cpp
regmodel::Register32 ctrl{CTRL_RMASK, CTRL_WMASK, CTRL_RESET};
...
case CTRL: data = ctrl.read(); break;   // read side
case CTRL: ctrl.write(data); break;      // write side
```

## Side-effect / W1C registers via callbacks (optional)

Registers with side effects can stay in the model via read/write callbacks
instead of an ad-hoc switch case:

```cpp
regmodel::Register32 intr_status{W1C_MASK, W1C_MASK, 0};
intr_status.on_write([](uint32_t cur, uint32_t in){
    return regmodel::apply_w1c(cur, in, W1C_MASK);
});
```

Registers with genuinely complex behavior (FIFO ports, HCI command/response
descriptors, DLAB-style banking) may remain explicit `reg_read`/`reg_write`
cases -- but still use the `regmodel` free functions for the masking
arithmetic. Do not force such registers into `Register<Word>` if it hurts
clarity.

See `doc/register-modeling-smc-vs-sep-csml.adoc` for why this exists instead
of adopting SEP's CSML framework.

## Offset dispatch: use `reg_map.h` where applicable

When a model has **plain** registers (RO/WO/RW mask contract only, or simple
W1C via `Register<Word>` write callbacks) with **no address banking** and
**no bus-level side effects** in the decode path, use
`common/include/reg_map.h` (`regmodel::RegisterMap<Word>`) instead of
hand-written `switch` cases for every offset.

Add `common/include` to `target_include_directories` and
`#include "reg_map.h"` alongside `reg_access.h`.

### When to use RegisterMap

- Plain software-only registers (e.g. UART `SCR`, `ECR`, `ITR`)
- Registers whose side effects are expressible as `Register<Word>` callbacks
  (W1C, woset) and do not need extra logic in `reg_write` beyond the callback

### When NOT to use RegisterMap (keep explicit decode)

- **Banked decode** (UART DLAB remapping 0x00/0x04 to DLL/DLM)
- **FIFO / command ports** with queue push/pop side effects
- **Read-modify-write with hardware logic** triggered after the write
  (e.g. `ECR`/`ITR` that also call `update_interrupts()` — write through the
  map, then invoke side-effect helpers in `reg_write`)
- **Memory-like targets** (bootrom, scratchpad RAM) — no register map
- **Routers** (smc_fabric) — route tracing, not offset tables

### Pattern (reference: `smc/peripherals/uart`)

```cpp
#include "reg_access.h"
#include "reg_map.h"

// members:
regmodel::Register8 scr_{0xFFu, 0xFFu, 0x00u};
regmodel::RegisterMap8 regmap_;

// constructor:
regmap_.add(uart_cfg::OFF_SCR, "SCR", scr_)
       .add(uart_cfg::OFF_ECR, "ECR", ecr_);

// reg_read — try map first, fall back to switch for banked/behavioral regs:
if (uint8_t rv = 0; regmap_.read(off, rv)) { data = rv; return true; }
switch (off) { ... }

// reg_write — map for plain regs; side-effect hooks after write when needed:
case uart_cfg::OFF_ECR:
    regmap_.write(off, b);
    update_interrupts();
    break;
```

Use `regmap_.for_each()` for debug dumps and `regmap_.find(off)` for
back-door access when the register is in the map.

## Transaction logging: use `sim_log.h`

New or edited SystemC/TLM models should use `common/include/sim_log.h`
(`SIM_LOG_*` macros, namespace `simlog`) instead of raw `SC_REPORT_INFO` for
routine messages and per-transaction tracing. Add `common/include` to the
target and `#include "sim_log.h"`.

### Level guide

| Macro | Use for |
|-------|---------|
| `SIM_LOG_INFO` | Construction / CCI config summaries (once at elaboration) |
| `SIM_LOG_TRACE` | Per-transaction `b_transport` read/write (addr, data, route target) |
| `SIM_LOG_DEBUG` | Decode misses, filter denials, unexpected but non-fatal paths |
| `SIM_LOG_WARN` | Recoverable runtime issues (out-of-range API calls) |
| `SIM_LOG_FATAL` / `SC_REPORT_FATAL` | Elaboration-time config errors that must abort simulation |

Keep `SC_REPORT_FATAL` for CCI/elaboration invariant checks that testbenches
depend on. Do not replace those with `SIM_LOG_FATAL` unless the test contract
is updated.

### Pattern (reference: peripherals and `smc_fabric`)

```cpp
#include "sim_log.h"

// constructor:
SIM_LOG_INFO(this, "uart instantiated: tx_fifo=" << cfg_.tx_fifo_depth);

// b_transport:
if (gp.is_read()) {
    ok = reg_read(addr, data);
    SIM_LOG_TRACE(this, "read  off=0x" << std::hex << addr << " data=0x" << data);
} else if (gp.is_write()) {
    ok = reg_write(addr, data);
    SIM_LOG_TRACE(this, "write off=0x" << std::hex << addr << " data=0x" << data);
}
if (!ok) SIM_LOG_DEBUG(this, "TLM decode miss at off=0x" << std::hex << addr);

// routers (smc_fabric): trace ingress, remap decision, and downstream target
SIM_LOG_TRACE(this, ingress << " " << cmd << " orig=0x" << std::hex << orig
                            << " -> " << (local ? "local" : "outbound"));
```

Runtime detail is controlled via `simlog::set_level(simlog::level::trace)` (or
a CCI `log_level` param wired to that call). At default `info` level, TRACE/DEBUG
are silent (one integer compare each).
